+# Route Planner Integration Contract
+
+Two waypoint route planners are provided as single-file ROS 2 (rclpy)
+nodes. They have no dependencies beyond `rclpy`, `nav_msgs`, and
+`geometry_msgs`, and are not packaged — run them directly or package
+them as you see fit:
+
+```bash
+python3 planner_a.py --ros-args -p route:="30,0,10; 30,30,10; 0,30,10"
+python3 planner_b.py --ros-args -p route:="30,0,10; 30,30,10; 0,30,10"
+```
+
+| | `planner_a.py` | `planner_b.py` |
+| - | -------------- | -------------- |
+| Node name | `route_planner_alpha` | `route_planner_beta` |
+| Path shape | straight segments | spline-smoothed curves |
+
+Both planners share the interface below.
+
+## Subscribed topics
+
+| Topic | Type | Notes |
+| ----- | ---- | ----- |
+| `~/odom_in` | `nav_msgs/Odometry` | Robot state. The first message anchors the route; the planner does nothing until it arrives. |
+
+## Published topics
+
+| Topic | Type | Notes |
+| ----- | ---- | ----- |
+| `~/planned_path` | `nav_msgs/Path` | Dense route (~`spacing_m` between poses) from the robot's current position through the remaining waypoints, republished at `replan_rate_hz`. `header.frame_id` is copied from the incoming odometry. After the final waypoint is reached, a single-pose path holding it. |
+
+QoS on both topics: RELIABLE, VOLATILE, depth 10 (rclpy defaults).
+
+## Parameters
+
+| Parameter | Type | Default | Meaning |
+| --------- | ---- | ------- | ------- |
+| `route` | string | *(required)* | Ordered waypoints `"x,y,z; x,y,z; ..."` in meters. |
+| `relative` | bool | `true` | If true, the route is interpreted relative to the robot pose at the first odometry message (x forward along heading, z up). If false, waypoints are taken verbatim in the odometry frame. |
+| `spacing_m` | double | `1.0` | Approximate distance between consecutive published poses. |
+| `arrival_radius_m` | double | `2.0` | A waypoint is considered reached (and dropped from the plan) when the robot is within this distance of it. |
+| `replan_rate_hz` | double | `2.0` | Publish rate of `~/planned_path`. |
+
+## Notes
+
+- Topic names above are the planners' own; remap them to whatever your
+  system expects.
+- The planners are pure path generators: they do not command motion,
+  monitor tracking, or avoid obstacles. Making the vehicle follow the
+  published path is outside their scope.
+- `use_sim_time` is supported the standard ROS 2 way
+  (`--ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true`).
